# len

[len](https://github.com/ewiger/len) — "a meta programming language" — is the
experimental notation this project uses to write a [[model-lenses|lens]] down
formally instead of only in prose. The rhyme is intentional: `len` is the
notation, a *lens* is the artifact it produces.

*len* is **experimental**. It is not a settled language with a frozen grammar; it
is a moving target, and this project tracks the parts of it that are stable
enough to use. In practice that means we work almost entirely at its two lowest
levels — **natural language plus relational logic** — and treat the higher,
fully formal end as an aspiration rather than a daily tool.

## Levels of formalism

The central idea of *len* is that one model can be captured at increasing rigor,
so a lens starts loose and is tightened as it stabilizes. The levels are a
spectrum, not separate documents:

- **L0 — Natural Spec.** Human intent: goals, worked examples, rationale, edge
  cases, informal pseudo-code. This is ordinary [[behavior-driven-development]]
  documentation — natural language, often with diagrams — written in *len*'s
  vocabulary. The loosest lens.
- **L1 — Structural Core.** The canonical model expressed in **relational
  logic**: types, relations, invariants, contracts, laws, and function
  obligations (`requires` / `ensures`), each with worked examples. Currently
  *len*'s most stable layer, and the deepest one we usually reach.
- **L2 — Generation Contract.** How the abstract model maps to concrete output:
  package layout, naming rules, AST shapes, runtime choices, error strategy,
  performance targets, pseudo-code. In progress upstream; rarely used here yet.

Because L0 and L1 share one vocabulary, an informal card and a formal `.len`
file describe the same model in the same terms and can be refined into each
other. That is why an L0 [[model-lenses|lens]] written as an `.hmd` card is still
a lens: it is the natural-language end of this same ladder.

## Why we use it: tests and proofs

The reason *len* earns its place here is not formalism for its own sake — it is
**test generation**. Our focus is turning a lens into checks a machine can run:

- A lens states obligations (`requires` / `ensures`) and laws. Each obligation is
  a concrete, checkable claim, so writing the lens *is* the specification a test
  suite verifies. In this project, **lenses are how we do
  [[test-driven-development]]**: the L1 core is written before, and drives, the
  code under `src/`.
- Tightening a lens is deepening a proof. An L0 example is an anecdote; an L1 law
  is a property; and the intended ceiling is a lens formal enough to carry a
  [[mathematical-proof]] — formalism deepening from an L0 sketch toward a
  machine-checkable specification.

So *len* is our way to **generate proofs**, with test cases as the pragmatic,
runnable first rung of that ladder.

## In grem

grem does not run or check *len*; it only places lenses. The
[`doc/lenses`](../../../.grem/styles/doc/lenses/prompt.md) documentation style
turns a source doc into an L0/L1 lens under the right facet of `doc/models/`,
emphasizing the obligations and laws that become tests.

See [[model-lenses]] for what a lens is and where lenses live, and [[grem-cli]]
for how grem stamps `doc/models/`.
